<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Hosts the real API request pipeline in process, which requires the API's
    Program class to be public and partial in the global namespace. Every test
    class here must carry [Trait("Category", "Integration")], because that is
    what the "Category=Integration" test filter selects.

    Test-database strategy: every run uses a real SQL Server. TestDatabaseFactory
    selects the route, and it needs NO PREPARATION to do so — the acceptance gates
    are run verbatim, so a run that configures nothing must still provision a
    database. Order: DNN_TESTS_USE_MSSQL truthy starts a throwaway container;
    otherwise DNN_TEST_SQLSERVER names an existing server; otherwise a falsey
    DNN_TESTS_USE_MSSQL refuses and the run fails closed; otherwise a reachable
    container runtime is discovered and used; otherwise the run fails closed with
    one actionable diagnosis. Discovery is why the bare gate command works on an
    unprepared host, and it can only fire when nothing was configured.

    No fixture selects the InMemory or Sqlite provider, and none can: the credential
    store answers "unavailable" on any provider that is not SQL Server, the accounts
    these tests sign in as live in the external aspnet_* tables that no model-driven
    creation could produce, a query root arrives through FromSqlInterpolated, and the
    persistence suite asserts declared column types against the relational catalogue.
    Account creation answering 201 is an acceptance criterion and fails closed
    without all of that. The two references below are RETAINED because AAP 0.6.1
    approves them for this project and the approved set is not narrowed here — but
    they are unexercised, deliberately and visibly so, and the capability they were
    meant to provide (a gate that runs with no database prepared) is provided by
    discovery instead. Adopting either one later means authoring a SQLite-dialect
    schema, a substitute for the external membership store and provider-aware
    catalogue expectations; it is a deliberate piece of work rather than a switch.
    The disposition is recorded in MIGRATION_NOTES.md.

    ANALYZER HAZARD: xunit.analyzers ships transitively with xunit and reports its
    xUnit#### rules as warnings, which the inherited warnings-as-errors policy
    promotes to build errors. Fix them in the test — never add an xUnit#### code
    to a NoWarn property, to backend/.editorconfig or to a new config file. The
    one that bites most often is the ban on blocking a Task inside a test, so
    every test method is `async Task`, never `async void`. Restore diagnostics are
    promoted the same way and are fixed by aligning versions.

    THREE EF CORE PROVIDERS ARE REACHABLE: SqlServer transitively through
    Infrastructure, plus the InMemory and Sqlite references below. Only one
    provider may be applied to a single DbContextOptions, so a fixture must remove
    the existing provider registration before adding its own rather than layering
    one on top.

    THE PACKAGE LIST BELOW IS CLOSED AT ELEVEN AND MUST STAY THERE. Ten entries are
    the set AAP 0.6.1 approves for this project, and a package that merely looks
    necessary is not thereby approved. The eleventh is a security pin, described
    next, and it is the only reason the count is not ten.

    SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3 is pinned to 2.1.12 to clear CVE-2025-6965
    (GHSA-2m69-gcr7-jv3q, high), which covers SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3 at every
    version up to and including 2.1.11. Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite 8.0.29
    selects 2.1.6, so the version EF Core asks for is vulnerable and no patched
    release exists on that line; 2.1.12 is the first unaffected 2.1.x. The bundle is
    pinned rather than lib.e_sqlite3 alone so that core, provider and lib all move
    together and the native family cannot skew across versions. This mirrors the
    Microsoft.Data.SqlClient 5.2.3 hardening pin that AAP 0.6.1 already applies for
    the same reason. Verified by restoring with NuGetAuditMode=all and
    NuGetAuditLevel=low, which reports no advisory once the pin is in place.

    The pin's cost is real and is accepted deliberately. A direct PackageReference
    wins version resolution outright, so this moves the native provider off the line
    the EF Core 8.0.29 band was tested against. It is safe here only because nothing opens a SQLite
    connection: no UseSqlite call exists anywhere in the repository, so the native
    e_sqlite3 asset is never loaded and a load or behaviour regression has no path to
    reach a test. If a fixture ever does adopt Sqlite, re-validate this pin against
    that fixture first, and prefer aligning the EF Core version over deepening the
    pin. Removing the Sqlite reference would remove the need for this pin as well -
    and is NOT the resolution taken, because AAP 0.6.1 approves that reference
    explicitly; the disposition is recorded in MIGRATION_NOTES.md.

    Testcontainers.MsSql is pinned to the plan's 3.10.0 rather than to 4.13.0, which
    AAP 0.6.4 lists among the versions checked and rejected. An earlier revision
    raised it to 4.13.0 to shed a transitive identity; the move is reverted because
    the 3.10.0 graph audits clean at mode=all and level=low, every identity it
    resolves - Docker.DotNet, SSH.NET, SshNet.Security.Cryptography, SharpZipLib,
    Newtonsoft.Json - is already claimed by a NuGet.Config source-mapping pattern,
    and the route was re-verified at runtime against this host's Docker Engine
    rather than assumed compatible.

    The production SQL client carries a direct pin too, but at the version the
    frozen dependency inventory names - Microsoft.Data.SqlClient 5.2.3 (AAP 0.6.1) -
    and for the reason the inventory gives: without the pin the SQL Server provider
    resolves an older 5.1.x line transitively. An earlier revision raised that pin to
    6.1.6 on its own initiative; it was withdrawn, because raising a version the plan
    pins is not this project's decision to take unilaterally. Its own reasoning is
    recorded in DnnMigration.Infrastructure.csproj beside the reference itself.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="FluentAssertions" Version="6.12.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" Version="8.0.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory" Version="8.0.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite" Version="8.0.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Moq" Version="4.20.72" />
    <PackageReference Include="SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3" Version="2.1.12" />
    <PackageReference Include="Testcontainers.MsSql" Version="3.10.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The three schema scripts are embedded rather than copied so that the suite carries its
    own database definition inside the test assembly and cannot be run against a
    half-provisioned directory. DnnSchema.sql holds the mapped legacy tables;
    MembershipSchema.sql holds the external aspnet_* objects that no mapped entity type
    describes and that AAP 0.7.1.3 shows the legacy DDL chain only ever ALTERs;
    HostSettingsSchema.sql holds the HostSettings table, which is likewise unmapped and is
    read through IHostSettingsService rather than through an entity type.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Schema\DnnSchema.sql" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Schema\MembershipSchema.sql" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Schema\HostSettingsSchema.sql" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Exactly one project reference: Application, Infrastructure and Domain arrive
    transitively through the API, and naming them would blur the dependency
    direction the reference graph exists to enforce.

    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing supplies the ASP.NET Core framework
    reference needed to host the API in process, so this project stays on
    Microsoft.NET.Sdk and adds no FrameworkReference of its own.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DnnMigration.Api\DnnMigration.Api.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
